<article class="env-lemma" id="084X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/084X">Lemma <span data-tag="084X">70.6.5</span>.</a> Notation and assumptions as in Situation <a href="/tag/084W" data-tag="084W">70.6.1</a>. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is universally injective, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f_0$ is locally of finite type, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $f_ i$ is universally injective for some $i \geq 0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Recall that a morphism $X \to Y$ is universally injective if and only if the diagonal $X \to X \times _ Y X$ is surjective (Morphisms of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/03MV" data-tag="03MV">67.19.3</a> and Lemma <a href="/tag/040X" data-tag="040X">67.19.2</a>). Observe that $X_0 \to X_0 \times _{Y_0} X_0$ is of locally of finite presentation (Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/084P" data-tag="084P">67.28.10</a>). Hence the lemma follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/07SN" data-tag="07SN">70.6.4</a> by considering the morphism $X_0 \to X_0 \times _{Y_0} X_0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>